course: dictionary
lesson: 11027
title: Waxy surfaces
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Waxy means made of wax, covered in wax, or looking or feeling like wax. It is smooth, shiny and a little slippery. Plants use a waxy coat to save water.
explain: «Pine needles are thin, tough and waxy, so they lose little water in winter when the ground is frozen.» The word waxy tells you the needles have a coat that keeps water in. Frozen ground gives the tree little water, so the coat helps it survive.
explain: Context gives clues again. «A thick waxy skin keeps water in» tells you that the cactus's skin is a protective layer. Ask what job the skin does, and the word waxy starts to make sense.
explain: Waxy can also describe a face. In a story, a very pale, smooth face may be called waxy, which means the person looks ill. The same word can mean a little different things, so always check the words around it.
fact: Waxy means made of wax, covered in wax, or feeling like wax.
fact: «Waxy leaves», «waxy skin» and «waxy coating» are common phrases in science lessons.
fact: A waxy coating helps a leaf keep water in and lets rain roll off.
fact: Some bristlecone pine needles stay on the tree for over 40 years.
fact: A pond skater's light, waxy feet do not break the water's skin.
exercise: «Pine needles are thin, tough and waxy, so they lose little water in winter.» What does waxy mean here? | Heavy and wet | Loud and crackly like paper | *Covered in a smooth coat that keeps water in | Sweet to taste
exercise: Why does a «waxy coating» help a leaf? | It makes the leaf taste sweet | *It stops water from leaving the leaf | It makes the leaf turn bright red | It lets the leaf fly away
exercise: In «Thin needles with a waxy coat help evergreen trees lose little water in winter», find and copy the word that describes the coat. | evergreen | trees | *waxy | thin
test: In a story, «Her face was pale and waxy, and she lay very still.» What does waxy suggest? | She is making a candle | She is laughing at a joke | She has just eaten a sweet | *She looks ill
test: «A pond skater's light, waxy feet do not break the water's skin.» Why can the skater stand on the water? | *Its feet are light and waxy, so they do not break the skin | Its feet are sticky like glue | It can fly over the water | It has a heavy shell
exam: Why do many cacti open their stomata only at night? | Because the sun makes them sleepy | Because the night makes their spines grow | Because bees only visit at night | *Because it is cool and much less water is lost
